- At `-O2`, the compiler may optimise both functions, making their execution times similar.
- The `inline` keyword does not guarantee better performance.

The actual timings depend on the computer and compiler, so they must be measured rather than assumed.

## 8. Alternative Concept – Member Functions and Getters

Instead of using friend functions, the comparison can be performed using member functions.

Example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Time
{
private:
    int h, m;

public:
    Time(int hh, int mm)
    {
        h = hh;
        m = mm;
    }

    int minutesTo(const Time& b)
    {
        int t1 = h * 60 + m;
        int t2 = b.h * 60 + b.m;

        int diff = t1 - t2;

        if (diff < 0)
            diff = -diff;

        return diff;
    }

    bool isEarlier(const Time& b)
    {
        if (h < b.h)
            return true;

        if (h == b.h && m < b.m)
            return true;

        return false;
    }
};

int main()
{
    Time departure(9, 45), arrival(13, 10);

    cout << departure.minutesTo(arrival) << ' '
         << boolalpha << departure.isEarlier(arrival);

    return 0;
}
```

Here, the functions are called using an object and the dot operator.

Another alternative is to provide public getter functions for hours and minutes. Normal external functions can then use those getters to compare the objects without directly accessing private data.

### Complexity Analysis

All three approaches perform a fixed number of operations.

- Friend functions: O(1) time per call.
- Member functions: O(1) time per call.
- Getter functions: O(1) time per call.

The extra space used by each approach is O(1).

### Comparison

| Method | Access to private data | Encapsulation |
|---|---|---|
| Friend functions | Direct access | Private data is maintained |
| Member functions | Direct access | Private data is maintained |
| Public getters | Access through functions | Private data is maintained |

The time complexity remains O(1) for all three approaches. The actual execution time should be compared using the required compiler settings.

## 9. Deployment Decision

I would keep the friend functions for this train journey planner because the comparison involves two `Time` objects equally.

They can directly access the private hours and minutes without making the data public.

However, member functions are also a good choice if the comparison is considered an operation belonging to one `Time` object.

## 10. Conclusion

This task helped me understand friend functions and inline functions in C++. Friend functions can access private members of a class, while member functions belong to the class itself. Inline functions may reduce function-call overhead for small operations, but the compiler decides whether to inline them.
